<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ha Tien Principality</h1><p class="st-meta">1707 CE – 1832 CE (approx.) · capital Ha Tien</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A semi-independent trading port on the Gulf of Thailand founded by the Chinese exile Mac Cuu, which bridged Cambodia, Vietnam and Siam.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-cuu">Mac Cuu</a>, a Ming loyalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Ha Tien</a>, then a Cambodian coastal town called Peam, in the late 17th century and developed it into a trading port with Cambodian approval. Around 1707 to 1708 he submitted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a>, who confirmed him as governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Ha Tien</a> was run by the Mac family as a hereditary principality with its own army and fleet, trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and goods across the Gulf with Siam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-thien-tu">Mac Thien Tu</a> it became a cultural centre where Chinese and Vietnamese poets gathered, and its rulers intervened in Cambodian successions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Ha Tien</a> in 1771, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son</a> wars further damaged it. Members of the Mac family continued as governors under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty</a> into the early 19th century, and the territory was fully incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam's</a> provincial system by 1832.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ha-tien-principality.jpg" alt="Ha Tien Principality" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Trương Thành Ý · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quang_truong_ha_tien.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1707 CE – 1832 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ha Tien</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mac Cuu, Mac Thien Tu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese, Vietnamese, Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hà Tiên, Kang Kao, Mang Kham</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1770&amp;focus=ha-tien-principality" aria-label="Ha Tien Principality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7561 1788 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ha Tien Principality lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7881 2044L7880 1991M7881 2044L7930 1822M7881 2044L7930 1822"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7880 1991h0M7930 1822h0M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7881 2044h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7881 2044h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1770&amp;focus=ha-tien-principality">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
